text:
/// <summary>
/// Audits every user command (20/06 §6, M06): success is written in the same transaction; denied/failed is written in a separate transaction
/// (the main transaction has rolled back). Internal commands without a user (heartbeat, scheduler) are not recorded.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Syncs builtin recipes + the task catalog from the release's `recipes/` directory (M17 §2, D-30).
/// Each manifest goes through selfcheck (M17 R2a). State: `unbuilt` (selfcheck passed, awaiting CI build + smoke) or `invalid`.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// A cached CPU-only inspection of a recipe; <c>Blocks</c> is a JSON array of <c>{key, params, trainable}</c>, <c>Sources</c> a JSON object
/// pane → <c>[{name, file, line, code}]</c> (the upstream code the merged config selects).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Rules of the recipe inspection (round 1 slice 2, D-44): which parameters make a new cache entry, the command the image runs
/// (<c>canvas.inspect</c> of the manifest) and how its one <c>MLP_INSPECT {json}</c> line is read.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The shell command of <c>canvas.inspect</c>: <c>{variant.x}</c> from the variant, <c>{dataset.classes.count}</c> = the class count
    /// (0 = keep the variant's own), <c>{params.json}</c> = every set value, <c>{params.x}</c> = the set value or the manifest default.
    /// </summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// The values a training would run with: what was set, plus every non-null manifest default. Without the defaults the inspection would
    /// show the variant's own schedule (e.g. 132 epochs) while the training scales it to the recipe default (72).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// <c>sources_gz</c> = base64 of gzipped JSON <c>{pane: [{name, file, line, code}]}</c>. Only that shape is kept; anything unreadable
    /// gives <c>{}</c> (the canvas then has no Code level for the pane).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Internal (launcher, environment builds): records the state of an image on a machine. <c>building</c> starts the clock;
/// <c>ready</c> / <c>failed</c> on a machine with no record yet also creates one (the launcher built or found the image itself).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Content of a dataset version (or of the working copy when <c>VersionId</c> is null): folders and files under a prefix with
/// browser-loadable URLs, the YOLO label file paired with each image, and the labels made in the platform (M05).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Host dataset cache: one docker volume per dataset per machine (<c>mlp-data-{dataset}</c>) holding immutable objects; each run gets a view of
/// hard links to them. A file is cached by an id derived from an address whose bytes can never change: dataset bucket keys are written once
/// (version numbers are never reused — <c>data.datasets.version_seq</c>) and Hugging Face links pinned to a commit. Other references (plain
/// URLs, external S3) can be overwritten by their owner, so they get no id and are downloaded for every run.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Remove files from what the next version will contain: a file added since the latest version is dropped at once (and its upload deleted);
/// a file of the latest version is marked, and leaves with the next version. Versions themselves never change.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Delete a dataset version that nothing uses (no run trained on it, no labeling project, draft or evaluation points at it). Its stored
/// files are deleted too, unless a later version still points at them.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Delete a dataset: it leaves every list and its labeling projects end. Versions that trained runs stay readable for those runs
/// (a run keeps showing which data it used), so nothing is erased from storage here.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Commit a version (M04 R5, R6): the latest version, minus the paths marked for removal, plus the files added since (the working copy).
/// Added files are copied to the version's own `v{n}/` folder; files that stay point at where they already are (no copy). The result is
/// immutable, and the working copy is empty again — it always holds only what changes in the next version.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Reference version: records the files' source addresses without copying anything; the machine downloads them from the source
/// when a job uses the version. Immutability comes from the source being pinned (HF commit sha, fixed URLs/keys).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Fixed dataset splits (D-49). The same rule lives in <c>recipes/_shared/converters/to_coco.py</c>: an image's split is
/// <c>sha256("{seed}:{key}")</c> (first 8 bytes, big endian) mod 10 000, compared with the ratios — so it depends on the path only.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Makes a new version with a fixed train/val/test split: the same files (referenced, not copied) plus <c>mlp-split.json</c>.
/// Group "folder" keeps every image of one directory in the same split (frames of one video).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Materialize (M07 §5): reads the source dataset version's CSV → offline store (full history by event time) → online store (latest row per entity).
/// Fully rebuilt each time (idempotent). Data errors → view `failed` + reason, no throw (so the state is saved).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Starts an import from HF Hub. Dataset: creates a new dataset if none is selected (task required); Model: checks model.write permission.
/// A worker in the api downloads files from HF and streams them straight to S3 storage (no temporary copy).
/// </summary>
/// <summary>
/// Import into a dataset or model. Source: "hf" (Repo = org/name, Include = globs), "url" (Include = https URLs, Repo unused),
/// "s3" (Repo = s3://bucket/prefix in our storage). Mode: "copy" = files are copied into our storage now; "reference" = only the
/// addresses are recorded and machines download from the source when a job uses the version.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Admin account declared in config (MLP__Admin__Email/Password/Name — compose `.env`): api ensures it exists on every startup.
/// Instance not set up → complete setup with this account (skipping /setup); set up but the account is missing → create an Instance Admin + personal workspace.
/// If it already exists, leave it unchanged (do not overwrite a password the user changed). Returns true when just created.
/// </summary>

/*
 * Hermes label checks (phase 2). The analysis itself (rules → decision model) runs in the api (HermesLabelChecker);
 * this layer loads what it needs with the caller's rights, stores each check with all its steps, applies auto-accept
 * inside a locked transaction, and lets a Lead/Reviewer undo it. Nothing changes an item without a stored, visible check.
 */

/// <summary>
/// Auto-accept after a passing check (internal). Applies only if the item is still waiting for review at the checked revision —
/// otherwise nothing changes and the caller records why.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Bring a project up to the dataset's latest version: the project now labels that version and its new files become items. Items and labels
/// already made stay.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Existing labels of an item (the YOLO txt next to it in the version) become editable platform labels, once — the first time the item is
/// opened. Source "import". An item that already has platform labels is left alone.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Labels → a dataset version a recipe can train on: the labeled files, a YOLO `.txt` next to every image (`class cx cy w h`, normalized, class =
/// position in the schema), `data.yaml` + `classes.txt` with the class names, and `labels.jsonl` holding every label of any kind.
/// Only finished items go in: accepted, or every submitted item when <paramref name="AllLabeled"/>.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Gateway guardrails (M15 §4): PII (email, VN phone number, CCCD/CMND national ID, card) → off / redact / block; prompt injection (English + Vietnamese patterns) → block;
/// Admin-defined banned terms → block. Applied to input, and to output when `check_output` is on.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Drift statistics (M13 §2) — pure C#, no external library dependencies (AOT):
/// numeric → 10 bins by baseline deciles, PSI + two-sample KS; categorical → PSI over frequencies (including an "other" bucket).
/// Default PSI thresholds 0.1 (warning) / 0.25 (alert) per common practice.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Marks it stopping and returns the job so the host stops the container + Ray job (WorkloadLauncher.StopJobAsync).
/// Stop on a notebook that is already stopping = force stop: it is marked stopped right away (the stop is sent to the machine again),
/// so a machine that never answers can no longer leave the user stuck.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Restarts a stopped/failed notebook: a new job with the same config (the home volume is kept, so files remain), on the previous machine by default.
/// The image follows the current profile (picking up new images); an overridden image (dev) is kept.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Reaper: finishes "stopping" notebooks. Job already over (or gone) → stopped. Still not confirmed after 5 minutes → stopped with
/// a reason saying so; the returned jobs get the stop sent again.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Writes notifications in the same transaction as the source event (M16 §5; replaces the outbox for P1): skips users who turned that kind off (R3),
/// merges the same kind on the same resource within 5 minutes (R1). Links are relative web hash routes (R2: no sensitive data).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Cancel any job that has not finished — waiting, reserving, preparing or running — from the Jobs page. Whatever owns the job follows:
/// a training run or trial becomes cancelled, a deployment or test replica stopped, a notebook stopped. The host then stops the container
/// and frees the reservation (WorkloadLauncher.StopJobAsync).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Picks the cache volumes to delete: idle longer than <see cref="DataCacheJanitorRules.MaxIdle"/>, plus — on a machine whose free disk is below
/// <see cref="DataCacheJanitorRules.MinFreeBytes"/> — idle caches oldest first until enough is freed. Caches whose last job is active are never picked.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Estimate for every machine that has a cache of the dataset (machines without one would download everything). Cached = files of the version
/// whose cache id belongs to a whole version previously synced on that machine. Trial samples are not counted, so the estimate errs on the high side.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Scheduler: orders the machines that fit a job so the one already holding most of the job's dataset comes first (the data does not
/// travel again), and records the choice on the job's timeline. Machines without the data keep their original order after it.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Deletes a machine's cache of one dataset (also how a full re-download is forced). Refused while a job using it is active; the caller sends
/// the volume removal to the agent after this succeeds.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Authenticates the `mlpj_` workload token with fencing (20/10 §4.5): a token of a replaced/finished attempt → `SCHED_ATTEMPT_FENCED`,
/// so two copies never write to the same run.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Agent (restarted or reconnected) reports containers still running (20/10 §4.3, §4.7): `suspect` attempt with a container → `running` (nothing lost);
/// `suspect` attempt with no container → returned so the host finalizes `container_lost` (retry from checkpoint); container of a replaced attempt → stop.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Picks the restore point for a new attempt (20/10 §4.6): latest checkpoint; same machine with a local copy → use the volume; different machine → durable copy (presigned GET).
/// Returns the value for `MLP_RESUME_FROM` (`local:&lt;name&gt;` | `url:&lt;presigned&gt;|&lt;name&gt;`) or null.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Anomaly detector (20/10 §2.3): shim signal lost (warning; too long → return the attempt so the host stops and retries),
/// progress unchanged for longer than `stall_after_min` → "may be stalled" (warning only, no automatic stop).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Fleet autonomy (D-40): machines keep their containers running while the control plane is down, however long. When the control
/// plane starts again it must not judge them by clocks that kept ticking during the outage: every watchdog (waiting for a machine,
/// shim signal, stalled progress, idle notebook, idle test replica) restarts from now, and running attempts wait
/// <paramref name="Grace"/> for their agent to reconnect and report what is actually running.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Pipeline definition (M14 §2): `{"params":{…defaults}, "steps":[{"id","kind","needs":[…],"config":{…},"retries":0}]}`.
/// Steps: `dataset` (import from Hugging Face / URL / S3 into a dataset version + its classes), `train` (M09 run), `job` (M03 job),
/// `gate` (condition), `register` (M11 from run), `promote` (change stage).
/// Strings in config may reference `${params.x}` / `${steps.&lt;id&gt;.&lt;field&gt;}` (when the whole string is one reference → the original type is kept).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// One engine tick for a run (M14 §5): checks running steps (M09 run / M03 job), starts eligible steps, skips branches after a failed step/closed gate,
/// finalizes the run when every step has finished. The host dispatches it in a scope acting as the user who started the run (child commands are audited as that user).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// `dataset` step: the dataset named in config (slug from the name) is reused when it already has a version (reuse: false → import again);
    /// otherwise an import starts (default: Hugging Face, reference mode — files stay at the pinned source commit). `classes` (names, index = class id)
    /// becomes the dataset's label schema when it differs, so training gets {dataset.classes.*} and num_classes from it.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Model-agnostic evaluation math: how ground truth is found in a dataset version (YOLO, COCO, class folders) and the
/// standard metrics (COCO mAP@[.50:.95] with 101-point interpolation; precision/recall/F1 at a confidence threshold, IoU 0.5;
/// accuracy/macro F1/confusion matrix for classification).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Pairs images with their ground truth. Detection: YOLO txt next to the image (…/images/x.jpg ↔ …/labels|yololabels/x.txt,
    /// or the same folder) or one COCO json (handled by the caller); classification: the parent folder name is the class.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Formats built from the version's ONNX file (any recipe that exports ONNX) by <see cref="DerivedScript"/> in the generic-onnx image:
    /// FP16 ONNX for GPU runtimes, OpenVINO IR for Intel CPUs/iGPUs, TensorRT engines built on the target GPU (engines only run on that GPU model + driver).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Runs inside the recipe image (python + mlp-shim are there): downloads the model version to /mnt/model, lets the shim run the recipe's
    /// export command, then uploads the result — the single output file as it is, several files as one zip — and a small meta.json naming it.
    /// Passed in an environment variable so that changing it never rebuilds a recipe image.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// A running conversion whose job succeeded becomes <c>ready</c> (the uploaded file takes its real name), one whose job failed or was
/// cancelled becomes <c>failed</c> with the job's reason. Settled here, on read, so no scheduler hook is needed.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Copies into <c>models/{model}/v{n}/</c>: weights/model.onnx, config/onnx.json, classes.json (from the source), signature.json, report.json,
/// pipeline.py and SHA256SUMS. The version keeps the source's run so it shows in the project, and its device decides GPU or CPU when deployed.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Registers a model version from a run (M11 §5.1, R5): server-side copy of the run's model directory to
/// `mlp-artifacts/{ws}/models/{model}/v{n}/` (immutable, R1); final metrics and recipe/variant are recorded on the version (R7).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// D-50: one GPU runs several jobs. A job with one GPU reserves a memory budget on it instead of the whole device; the scheduler packs jobs
/// while the budgets fit. Nothing enforces the budget inside the container — it is a reservation, the same way CPU shares are.
/// The budget: what the job states, else by kind — deployment 2 GB, notebook 4 GB, recipe job = the recipe's (variant's) `minVramGb`
/// (half of it for anything that is not training). Unknown → the whole GPU, as before.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Attempt stuck in "reserving" longer than <paramref name="Timeout"/> (Ray has no suitable node, holder lost, machine out of resources…) → mark it failed
/// and requeue the job (not counted as a retry). Returns the attempt ids so the api cancels the Ray holder job.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Deploy model version (M12 §5.1, R1, R8): replica = `serve` job running `mlp-serve` in the recipe's image; the model directory is downloaded via
/// presigned GET (user = container); the handler comes from the recipe's `entrypoints.serve.handler`.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// "Test model": reuse a running replica of the model version, or start a test replica that stops itself after
/// <see cref="TestReplica.IdleStopMin"/> minutes without calls. Gpus &lt; 0 = follow the recipe (environment.gpu: required → 1 GPU).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Model files of a version as [{path, url}]: a reference version (imported by address) is read from its sources, otherwise from our storage.
/// Links last 24 hours, so the scheduler signs them again for every replica start.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Round 1 §8.6: can the numbers be compared (same validation set, same metric axis), and only the lines that differ.
/// <paramref name="Axes"/> per run: "step" (epoch metrics moved onto the step axis, R25) or "epoch" (runs from before).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Same val = every run trained on the same dataset version (its split is fixed, R8). Axis = where the primary metric's last point sits against
/// the epochs the run asked for: beyond them it is on the step axis. Rows: recipe, variant, data, start, kind, revision and every parameter whose
/// effective value (set, else the recipe default) differs.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// epochs × (⌊train images / batch⌋ / steps per second + ⌈val images / 64⌉ × seconds per val batch); −20 % … +50 %.
    /// Without a measured validation time only the training steps are counted, and the source says so.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Freezes the draft into a revision (shared by train and trial): the same content never makes a second revision.
    /// Returns the revision and its trial key.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Replaces the caller's draft: from a revision, from a run ("cook again from #n" — also its data, starting point and machine),
/// or from the Catalog recipe as it is now (which also moves the project's copy to that manifest).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The view of a trial. While it waits or runs, <paramref name="liveLines"/> (the attempt's <c>[mlp-trial]</c> lines from the log) give the stages,
    /// and a running job shows the trial as running.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The subset a trial trains on: per split, images in the order of their hash with seed "trial" — train = max(160, 10 × batch), val = 128
    /// (fewer when the split is smaller). The split comes from the version's <c>mlp-split.json</c>, else from train/val folders.
    /// Non-image files are kept when their stem matches a chosen image (labels) or no image at all (classes.txt, data.yaml, the split file).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// One trial. <paramref name="LiveLines"/> returns the <c>[mlp-trial]</c> lines the running attempt of a job has logged so far
/// (the host keeps the log; the application cannot read it itself).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// The hidden run's job finished (called next to <see cref="SyncRunStateCommand"/>): records the stages and measurements from the attempt's
/// <c>[mlp-trial]</c> lines, the machine it ran on, and passed / failed. <paramref name="Headline"/> = the error line of the log, if any.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// R30: the data links in a training's job spec are signed when the job is submitted and expire after 24 hours. Every attempt launch signs them
/// again (same files, same version — only the links change), so a run that queued for a day or retried after a long stage still downloads its data.
/// Returns the spec unchanged when it has no dataset version.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Train from the UI (M09 §5.1): recipe + variant + task + params → run + job running `mlp-shim` in the recipe's image.
/// The shim spec is passed via the `MLP_JOB_SPEC` environment variable (JSON) — the shim reads it itself (20/08 §5.1).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Trial mode of <see cref="StartTrainingCommand"/> (round 1 R15–R22): only <paramref name="Files"/> of the dataset version are handed to the shim,
/// the run is hidden (numbered -1, -2, …) and the shim runs its two short passes with <paramref name="Passes"/> (<c>entrypoints.train.trial</c>).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Delete a finished run with its metrics and file list. Refused while a registered model version still points at it —
/// that version is the record of where the model came from. Returns the storage prefix so the host removes the files.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Search space (M10 §2): each parameter is `{"values":[…]}` (discrete; grid enumerates all) or `{"min":a,"max":b,"log":bool,"int":bool}` (continuous; random only).
/// Parameters are generated deterministically from (seed, trial number) → rerunning a sweep with the same seed yields the same trial sequence.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// One orchestrator step (M10 §5): sync trial state/values from M09 runs → update the best trial → done when the budget is used up and every
/// trial has finished → if there is room (parallel), generate new trials via StartTraining on behalf of the sweep creator (the host sets the user for the scope).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// A control channel past hello: sequential writes via a queue, a read loop, 15 s heartbeat,
/// 3 silent intervals (45 s without receiving a byte) → close (20/04 §3). Used by both sides via a type parameter.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Generates explicit DI registration + a type-switch mediator dispatch (no reflection; 20/02 §4).
/// Output: <c>{RootNamespace}.Generated.CqrsRegistration.AddGeneratedHandlers()</c> and <c>GeneratedMediator</c>.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// [SqlQuery("Module/Name.sql")] partial method → typed ADO.NET code (20/02 §5.2). No reflection.
/// .sql file header: <c>-- mlp:query Name :one|:many|:exec|:scalar</c>. Parameters <c>@snake_case</c> ↔ method parameters.
/// Result columns ↔ constructor parameters of the returned record, by snake_case name.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Applies migrations <c>db/&lt;schema&gt;/yyyyMMddHHmm_slug.sql</c> in file-name order (20/02 §5.5).
/// One transaction per file with <c>lock_timeout 5s</c>. <c>platform.migrations</c> stores a checksum of the SQL with comments and
/// whitespace removed ("v2:…"), so editing comments is allowed but changing an applied statement fails.
/// Legacy checksums (raw file bytes) are upgraded to v2 automatically while the file is still byte-identical.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Manifest selfcheck (M17 R2a): every template renders with sample values, placeholders are in the 20/08 §4.1 catalog,
/// parameters are declared, `{input.*}` is declared, and no bare `{weights}` remains where it may be empty.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Standard template grammar (cookbook 20/08 §4):
/// <c>{x}</c>, <c>{x?TEXT}</c>, <c>{x|a=T1|b=T2|*=T}</c>, <c>{{</c>/<c>}}</c>.
/// An empty/null value in bare form is an error; in <c>?</c>/<c>|</c> form it is a valid falsy value.
/// Shell: values (not static text) are quoted if they contain characters outside <c>[A-Za-z0-9_./:=@%+,-]</c>.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// YAML → JsonNode using only YamlDotNet's event parser (no reflection serializer → AOT-safe).
/// Unquoted scalars are typed per the YAML 1.2 core schema: null/bool/int/float; everything else is a string.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Minimal, AOT-safe S3 client (D-34): SigV4 for requests and presigned URLs, path-style
/// (<c>{endpoint}/{bucket}/{key}</c>) so it works with SeaweedFS/MinIO/Ceph/real S3.
/// Presigned URLs are signed for <c>PublicEndpoint</c> (the address browsers/agents see) when set.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Agent channel TLS (P0-8, 20/04 §3): with <see cref="AgentOptions.CertPin"/> (SHA-256 of the control plane certificate, printed in the install command)
/// only that exact certificate is accepted, so self-signed certificates work; without a pin → normal CA chain validation.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Commands from the control plane (D-38): the agent is a container bridge with generic commands (container, image, file, script) and no notion of
/// Ray node / workload / network. Each command returns exactly one CommandResult.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Container ground truth (D-38): follows <c>docker events</c> for every <c>mlp.managed=1</c> container (start / die) and reports on the current
/// channel; on reconnect it sends a <see cref="ContainerSnapshot"/> (running + exited but not yet removed; the control plane removes them after recording).
/// Containers labeled <c>mlp.logs</c> (= attempt id) have their logs followed continuously. The agent keeps no business state.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Agent-side data channel (20/04 §3.2): a new TCP(+TLS) connection to the control plane, DataChannelHello → DataChannelAccept, then pipes bytes
/// both ways with 127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt; on the host (notebooks listen on loopback only, R2). At most 64 concurrent channels.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Container control via the Docker CLI (works the same on all 3 OSes). Agent v2 (D-38) does not know what a container is used for;
/// it only translates a <see cref="ContainerSpec"/> into <c>docker run</c>; every platform container has the label <c>mlp.managed=1</c>
/// and <c>mlp.spec</c> (spec hash) so <c>ContainerApply</c> knows when to keep it as is.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Logs to a file in the agent folder (a Windows Scheduled Task has no console). Opened per line with FileShare.ReadWrite|Delete;
/// no file lock is held between writes (a wrapping cmd.exe once held agent.log and made the next startup fail). Rotates at 10 MB.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Files + scripts in the agent's state folder (D-38). The control plane uses these generic operations for OTA: write the upgrade script,
/// run it detached from the agent via the service manager, read <c>status.txt</c>, write the confirmation file. Relative paths only, cannot escape the folder.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Run a script. <c>detached</c>: hand it to the service manager so the script survives the agent being stopped/its binary replaced:
    /// Linux <c>systemd-run</c>, macOS <c>launchctl submit</c>, Windows a one-time Scheduled Task as SYSTEM.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Continuous logs (20/10 §2.4): `docker logs -f -t` for each workload container → LogChunk in blocks of ≤ 64 KB or 1 s.
/// Connection lost → write to an on-disk spool (≤ 100 MB/attempt) and resend after reconnecting; the api deduplicates by source timestamp.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Agent builds per platform (D-37/D-38). The control plane holds the agent source; the <c>agent-builder</c> service builds on demand and caches:
/// <c>{AgentDir}/source-version</c>, <c>queue/{ver}_{rid}.json</c>, <c>cache/{ver}/{rid}/{mlp-agent, .sha256, build.json, build.log}</c>.
/// Download links are always HMAC-signed + time-limited (blogic-tunnels pattern), used by both the install command and OTA.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// TCP(+TLS) listener :7443 for the agent channel (20/04 §3). TLS is on when MLP__Agent__CertPath (PFX) is set;
/// dev/test allows plain TCP when MLP__Agent__AllowPlaintext=true.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// An agent that just (re)connected reports running/exited containers (20/10 §4.3, §4.7): finalize exited containers, re-adopt live ones,
    /// a suspect attempt that lost its container → container_lost (retry from the local checkpoint on the same host); containers of superseded attempts → stop.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Agent OTA (D-38, blogic-tunnels pattern): the control plane decides and orchestrates; the agent only offers generic operations (FileWrite, RunScript
/// detached from itself, FileRead). Steps: build the right RID (agent-builder) → generate a script (sh/ps1) with a 1-hour signed URL + SHA-256 → the agent runs
/// the script via the service manager → read status.txt every 3 s → once the new agent has been connected ≥ 30 s, write final-ok → the script cleans up and reports done;
/// without final-ok within 240 s the script restores the old version on its own. Running containers are not touched (the agent is only a bridge).
/// At most 4 hosts at a time; jobs are stored in the DB (tracking resumes after an api restart).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Removes host dataset cache volumes automatically (DataCacheJanitorRules): idle for two weeks, or idle and the machine is short of disk.
/// Every 30 minutes; only machines that are connected; each removal is recorded on the machine's event stream.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Finds the line of a container log that explains a failure, so the job, run or deployment can say "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'x'"
/// instead of "exited with code 1". Launchers (torchrun, subprocess) wrap the real error in their own exception — those wrappers are skipped.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// AppException → gRPC status + trailers with the reason code and field errors (20/03 §4, 20/07 §3).
/// Messages are always English; the web UI translates them (same template table as the rest of the UI).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Runs model evaluations (Models → Evaluate): ensures a replica of the model version (reused or a test replica), then for each
/// sample downloads the image + ground truth, calls the replica and stores the per-sample result; at the end the metrics are
/// computed from the stored samples. Resumable: samples already stored are skipped after an api restart.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Public REST inference gateway (M12 §5 contract; JSON is an allowed exception, 20/03 §1):
/// POST /v1/deployments/{ws}/{slug}/predict, header `Authorization: Bearer mlpd_…`.
/// Body: {"inputs":[{"tabular":{"a":1}} | {"text":"…"} | {"image_b64":"…","conf":0.25}]} → {"predictions":[…]} theo task.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// POST /v1/deployments/{ws}/{slug}/feedback: late ground-truth labels (M13): {"feedback":[{"prediction_id":"…","label":"1"} | {"prediction_id":"…","value":1.5}]}.
    /// </summary>

/*
 * Hermes label check (phase 2). Fast and visible:
 *   1. rules (instant): labels present, classes valid, shapes plausible, duplicates;
 *   2. decision model (≈ 0.3 s, typed answer with confidence) — for text items, whose content the model can read;
 *   3. auto-accept — only on submit, only when the project threshold is set, the verdict passes and the model is confident enough.
 * Every step is stored with its outcome, detail and time; the model call is an AI run anyone can open. Nothing is decided silently.
 */

/// <summary>
/// D-38: the agent is only a container bridge; the control plane assembles the spec for each role (label <c>mlp.role</c>):
/// <c>net</c> (WireGuard spoke mlp-net), <c>ray-node</c> (thin Ray worker), <c>workload</c> (attempt container; labels <c>mlp.attempt</c>
/// + <c>mlp.logs</c> so the agent follows its logs). Changing how a role runs = edit here, no new agent release.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Platform training layer (20/08 §6): mlp-shim (built by agent-builder for the host's architecture, fetched from the control plane through a signed
    /// link) and the dataset converters the shim runs before the recipe command.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// <c>recipes/{name}:current</c> → build command for this host: the recipe's Dockerfile and files (context = recipes dir, as in CI)
    /// plus the platform serve layer and, when <paramref name="shim"/> is given, the training layer. One image serves both training and serving.
    /// The tag carries a content hash (recipe files, platform layers, mlp-shim sha256 — not the signed link), so a changed recipe is rebuilt and an
    /// unchanged one is reused. Other images → null.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Build the mlp-net spoke on the host (P0-16): build the image from the control plane's Dockerfile, get the public key (the private key never leaves the volume),
    /// store the key for the hub, run the spoke. Returns true when the network is ready; a freshly recreated spoke → the old Ray node loses its namespace → remove it to rebuild.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Hugging Face Hub client (public REST): search models/datasets, repo info, file tree, file download. The workspace token (if any)
/// is sent to read gated/private repos. No other user information is sent to HF.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// HF import worker (one job at a time: bandwidth + CPU): downloads each file from HF and PUTs it straight to S3 storage via an internal signed URL (streamed, no
/// temp storage). Dataset: writes files into the dataset, then commits version "Imported from HF repo@rev". Model: D-28 model folder (<c>weights/…</c> +
/// <c>recipe.lock.json</c>), a version with source hf_hub using the hf-auto recipe. Size limit: <c>Hub:MaxImportBytes</c> (default 50 GiB).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// MCP server (Model Context Protocol, Streamable HTTP transport, JSON responses) at <c>POST /mcp</c>: external AI agents (Claude, Cursor,
/// custom agents) operate the platform with a user API key (<c>Authorization: Bearer …</c>). Every tool is one platform RPC called over the
/// local gRPC port with the caller's token, so permissions, validation and business rules are exactly the platform's own; input schemas are
/// generated from the proto messages. No delete operations are exposed.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Data channel (20/04 §3.2): the api sends OpenDataChannel{session, token, target} on the control channel; the agent opens a new TCP connection to :7443
/// with DataChannelHello → the api matches it by session, replies DataChannelAccept, then both sides switch to raw bytes. One-time token, expires in 30 s.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// HTTP `/nb/{id}/…` (M08 §5 step 2, R2): notebook owner only (others → 404); forwards HTTP + WebSocket over the data channel to
/// 127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt; on the host; the api attaches the Jupyter token itself and does not forward the platform session cookie.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Per-attempt log store (20/10 §2.4): keeps the latest 2,000 lines in memory for realtime viewing; writes everything to S3 storage in ~1 MB chunks
/// (`mlp-artifacts/{ws}/jobs/{job}/attempts/{attempt}/logs/{seq}.log`, line = "ts_ms\tcontent"). Deduplicated by source timestamp (resent after reconnect).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The trial lines of an attempt: from memory, or — after an api restart — from the stored log. Each line is a whole <c>[mlp-trial] {json}</c>.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// System health (20/10 §2.6): components (PostgreSQL, S3 storage, Ray head, agent channel) checked every 15 s; each background loop records its own heartbeat,
/// missing for more than 3x its period → `down` + a `system.loop_stalled` event.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// WireGuard overlay (P0-16, D-26). The hub is the <c>wireguard</c> container (image mlp-net); the api and ray-head share its network namespace.
/// The hub writes its public key to <c>WireGuard:HubKeyFile</c> (/net/hub.pub); the api writes the peer list to <c>WireGuard:PeersFile</c> (/net/peers),
/// which the hub syncs every 2 s. No hub configured → overlay off, everything runs as before (dev runs the api on the host).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Public subdomains under <c>Routes:Domain</c> (e.g. mlp.hexbox.vn, wildcard DNS + cert at Traefik):
///   nb-{id:N}.{domain}            → notebook (proxy /nb/{id}/…, notebook owner only; the session cookie is shared across *.{domain})
///   {slug}--{workspace}.{domain}  → deployment: POST /predict, /feedback (endpoint key mlpd_…)
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// The recipe image ("environment") per machine (round 1 slice 2, D-44). One place computes the image tag a recipe needs on a machine — the
/// same way for a training launch and for the canvas — builds the image on request without holding a GPU, runs the CPU-only inspection
/// (<c>canvas.inspect</c>) in it, and reads the recipe's files from the control plane's disk.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The tag the recipe needs today on a machine of this architecture, without waiting: null while mlp-shim is not built yet or when the
    /// recipe has no Dockerfile. Remembered for a few seconds (the tag hashes every file of the recipe).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The launcher built (or found) a recipe image on a machine, or failed to: remember it so the canvas knows where the environment is.
    /// Never throws — a launch must not fail because of this note.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// M03 §5 scheduling loop (2 s): take the highest-priority job → pick hosts → create attempt + `mlpj_` token →
/// submit Ray job `mlp_holder` to create the placement group (R1). When the holder reports it is fully allocated → <see cref="WorkloadLauncher"/>.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Workloads outlive the Ray head (D-40), but their reservation does not: after a head restart Ray believes every CPU and GPU is free and would
/// give a busy GPU to the next job. Every 10 s this compares active attempts with Ray and submits a `--rehold` holder for each one Ray no longer
/// knows. Scheduling pauses while Ray is unreachable and for a short settle time after a re-hold.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Build the recipe image on the host (first use) and return its tag; null after reporting a failure. Every step is an attempt event, so the
    /// job page shows what the machine is doing (a first D-FINE build downloads ~5 GB and can take 10–30 minutes).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// D-36 (20/10 §4.4): a `suspect` attempt past its wait for the host to return → `node_lost` → the scheduler retries (same or another host) from the checkpoint.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Replica probing (M12 R2/R5): every 5 s call ModelServer.Health; success → healthy, failure → degraded (the gateway drops it from routing).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// StreamBroker (P0-7, 20/02 §5.5): one `LISTEN mlp_ws` connection per api; PostgreSQL triggers send `NOTIFY` when a workspace's jobs/events/notifications
/// change → wake the `Watch*` streams waiting on that key (correct with multiple api replicas). Logs live in api memory,
/// so <see cref="LogStore"/> signals locally via <see cref="Pulse"/>. Streams still re-poll at least every 5 s in case a NOTIFY is lost.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// First time an item of a version is opened: the YOLO label file next to the image (imported datasets, exported label versions) becomes
    /// editable labels. A source that cannot be read leaves the item unlabeled; the next open tries again.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// One stream per tab (20/10 §2.7): my running work, system status, unread notification count, and, when subscribed to a job,
    /// that job's new events + logs. Wakes when StreamBroker signals (workspace NOTIFY / new attempt logs), re-polls at least every 5 s; sends only on change; reconnect → full snapshot first.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Sweep orchestrator (M10, D-21): every 3 s advances each running sweep in a scope acting as the sweep's creator; trials are ordinary M09 runs,
/// audited as that user's StartTraining. Trials do not run inside Ray Tune.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Host dataset cache (see DataCacheRules on the control plane). The dataset's volume is mounted at <c>spec.DataCache</c>:
/// <c>objects/ab/{id}</c> = one immutable file per cache id, <c>views/run-{run}/…</c> = this run's dataset tree as hard links to objects.
/// Only files whose object is missing (or was modified in place — size or stamp differ) are downloaded; a dataset that did not change
/// transfers nothing. Files without an id (references that may change at the source) are downloaded into the view every time.
/// Progress is reported in bytes with a <c>sync</c> detail (reused / to download / done / failed), and a final summary the control plane keeps.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Specification of one attempt (the control plane writes the JSON, the agent mounts it into the container; 20/08 §5.1).
/// Path fields are paths **inside the container** prepared by the agent/shim.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Metric collection without changing the repo (20/08 §5.3): incrementally reads (by offset) the sources declared in the manifest, mapping keys
/// via <c>metrics.map</c>. Call <see cref="Poll"/> periodically and once more when the process exits.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Training progress read from the recipe's own output lines (round 1 R24): `progress.pattern` gives epoch e/E, step i/n and the line's loss;
/// `progress.validatePattern` marks the validation sub-phase. Epochs are 0-based in the pattern (D-FINE prints "Epoch: [3/24]" for the 4th epoch).
/// ETA = measured seconds per epoch (training + validation) × epochs left; before a full epoch was seen, the step rate of the current epoch.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// `canvas.phases[].untilKey` (e.g. train_dataloader.collate_fn.stop_epoch) → the epoch each stage ends at, read from the generated config
    /// (`progress.config`) by the key's last segment — the platform writes that file itself, so a flat `name: value` line is enough.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Shim operations channel (20/10): standard progress (step changes sent immediately, within a step at most once per 5 s), 30 s heartbeat, two-tier checkpoints.
/// Network errors do not break training; they are only logged (unless the attempt was superseded: stop immediately so the run is not overwritten).
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Runs the entrypoint's command once and collects its metrics. <paramref name="extra"/> = config overrides the recipe reads from
    /// <c>MLP_EXTRA_JSON</c> (trial passes, denser progress lines) — never spliced into the command template.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Fleet autonomy (D-40): training finished but the control plane or storage is unreachable → keep the result on the run volume
    /// and retry with backoff (up to MLP_UPLOAD_WAIT_HOURS, default 24) instead of failing a finished run.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Download the dataset version's files (20/08 §5.1 step 2), 4 at a time. Large lists come from <c>dataFilesUrl</c> (an env var holds at most 128 KB).
    /// Files land on the run's volume (<c>{output}/.mlp/data</c>), so a retried attempt on the same machine only fetches what is missing.
    /// HTTP 429/503 (e.g. Hugging Face rate limits) pauses every download until Retry-After, then backs off. Failure → code 1 (the scheduler retries).
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// The recipe reads COCO (<c>data.input: coco</c>) → run the platform converter baked into the image (YOLO / COCO tree → COCO {train,val}).
    /// It links images instead of copying them and writes annotations with class ids 0..N-1 in the dataset's class order.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// `MLP_RESUME_FROM` (20/10 §4.6), one entry per line: `local:&lt;name&gt;` = checkpoint still on the run's volume (same host);
    /// `url:&lt;presigned&gt;|&lt;name&gt;|&lt;sha256&gt;` = durable copy in storage, downloaded to the right place and checked against its sha.
    /// Several names (R28: last.pth and best_stg1.pth) are all restored; the one resumed from is the first in the recipe's `checkpoints` order.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Sends metrics to TrackingService with the workload token (20/08 §5.1 step 6). Buffers while the network is down; resends on the next flush
/// (buffer limit 100,000 points). Enabled when MLP_API, MLP_RUN_ID and MLP_JOB_TOKEN are set.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
/// Trial mode (round 1 R15–R22): the same training on a small subset, in two natural passes — pass 1 ends after one stage-1 epoch,
/// pass 2 resumes from its checkpoint into stage 2 — then packaging and a reload of the model through the serve handler.
/// Every stage prints one <c>[mlp-trial] {json}</c> line when it starts and one when it ends; the control plane reads them from the job log.
/// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// Loads the packaged model through the recipe's serve handler (python -m mlp_serve.selftest) and predicts a few val images.
    /// Anything the shim cannot provide (no handler, no mlp_serve in the image) → skipped, not failed.
    /// </summary>

/// <summary>
    /// "Load model.state_dict, {'missed': [...], 'unmatched': [...]}" → layers loaded (unknown: the line does not list them) and layers created new
    /// (missed + unmatched). No such line → (null, null).
    /// </summary>
